{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786666902,"nonce":"1+pBEagOfME656I2LKWvBeAYILU=","title":"`Reconciler.FanOut` still walks every tenant inside one execution, the defect commit `2d699d6` closed everywhere else","message":"**What is wrong.** `Reconciler.FanOut` (`internal/sources/service/reconcile.go:637-657`) is the handler for `source.reconcile`'s zero-payload fan-out tick, and it still has the shape commit `2d699d6` (\"perf: a periodic sweep fans out per tenant instead of looping them all\") converted five other periodic handlers away from: `scopes, err := r.orgs.Scopes(ctx)` at `:642` reads every tenant with no page size and no ceiling, then `for _, scope := range scopes { n, err := r.fanOutTenant(ctx, scope); ... }` (`:648-655`) runs `fanOutTenant` once per tenant inside that one call. `fanOutTenant` (`:660-687`) is itself a second, bounded fan-out — `due, err := r.sources.repo.ListDue(ctx, scope, FanOutLimit)` (`:663`) caps sources per tenant — but it enqueues two jobs per due source, `SourceReconcileArgs` and `SilencesSyncArgs` (`:673-680`), so the whole call is a two-level fan-out whose inner level is bounded and whose outer level — the one that scales with tenant count — is not.\n\nThe tick that runs this is not slow-but-bounded, it is fast and frequent: `internal/app/workers.go:31`, `const reconcileFanOutInterval = 30 * time.Second`, wired to the periodic at `internal/app/workers.go:49-55`. The per-execution timeout is looser than the tick — `internal/platform/jobs/registry.go:202-203` registers `KindSourceReconcile` at `Timeout: 60 * time.Second` — so the accurate claim is \"one execution, ticked every 30 seconds, with a 60-second hard timeout,\" not \"one 30-second execution\": the 30 seconds is the tick interval and the `SourceReconcileArgs.InsertOpts` uniqueness window (`internal/platform/jobs/args.go:325-330`, `periodicOpts(QueueReconcile, PriorityNormal, 30*time.Second)`), and the 60 seconds is how long any one fan-out run actually has before it is killed mid-loop.\n\nThe nearby comment at `:634-636` — \"One tenant's failure logs and CONTINUES. The tick repeats within thirty seconds, which makes 'carry on' strictly better than 'abort'\" — is about per-tenant error handling, not about a bound on how many tenants one execution walks; it does not claim, and there is no comment anywhere in this file that claims, a ceiling on the outer `Scopes()` loop. That is the same absence `2d699d6`'s five conversions closed elsewhere in this codebase, and its own commit message names this file as the one left over: the fix converted five periodic handlers to a per-tenant fan-out with a keyset continuation and a ceiling, and made a point of leaving `effectiveRetention` whole because it is a reduce — `FanOut` is not a reduce, it is exactly the same MAP-shaped sweep the other five were, just not converted in that change.\n\n**The demonstration.** No load test was run; the shape is read directly. `internal/sources/service/reconcile.go:642`: `scopes, err := r.orgs.Scopes(ctx)` — the same unbounded-list call that `db.TenantLister.Scopes` exposes everywhere else in the codebase, and the one the five converted handlers stopped calling directly from inside their own execution. `:648-655`: the `for` loop over `scopes` with no `TenantFanOutLimit`-style cap and no continuation — every tenant in the deployment is visited before `FanOut` returns, on every 30-second tick, inside a 60-second timeout.\n\n**Why it matters.** A thousand-tenant deployment pays a thousand sequential `fanOutTenant` calls — each one a `ListDue` query plus (on any due sources) an `EnqueueMany` — inside one execution of a job that is scheduled every 30 seconds and killed at 60. That is precisely the failure mode `2d699d6`'s commit message describes for the five handlers it did convert: \"a thousand-tenant deployment paid a thousand sequential sweeps inside one timeout, and none of it shrank when a customer left.\" Here the same shape means the reconcile fan-out either falls behind its own 30-second schedule as tenant count grows, or is killed by the 60-second timeout partway through the tenant list — and because there is no keyset continuation, a run that is killed mid-loop does not resume where it stopped; the next tick starts over from `Scopes()`'s first tenant, so tenants late in the list can be starved of the pass that discovers their due sources.\n\n**Where.** `internal/sources/service/reconcile.go:637-657` (`FanOut`), `:642` (unbounded `Scopes()`), `:648-655` (the per-tenant loop with no ceiling or continuation), `:660-687` (`fanOutTenant`, the bounded inner level: `:663` `ListDue(..., FanOutLimit)`, `:673-680` the two enqueues per due source), `:634-636` (the comment that addresses per-tenant failure, not an outer bound); `internal/app/workers.go:31` (`reconcileFanOutInterval = 30 * time.Second`), `:49-55` (the periodic registration); `internal/platform/jobs/registry.go:202-203` (60-second execution timeout for `KindSourceReconcile`); `internal/platform/jobs/args.go:325-330` (the matching 30-second uniqueness window). The now-existing target shape: `internal/platform/jobs/tenant_fanout.go:80-104` (`TenantFanOutLimit = 500`, \"how many tenants ONE fan-out execution may enqueue for\"), `:163-164` (`Cursor`, \"the last org id this execution reached\"), `:216` (`tenants.ScopePage(ctx, after, TenantFanOutLimit)`), `:229-232` (building the continuation `TenantFanOut{After: out.Cursor}` when the page is full).\n\n**Scope.** medium — it is a conversion onto an existing, already-tested shape (`jobs.TenantFanOut`) rather than a new design, but it touches a two-level fan-out (tenants, then sources-per-tenant) and its own args/registry wiring, and needs a test at the scale the other five conversions were tested at.\n\n**Done when.** `FanOut` (or the `source.reconcile` handler that calls it) is converted onto `jobs.TenantFanOut`/`TenantFanOutLimit` the way `occurrence.reap`, `group.close`, `flap.score`, `retention.prune` and `stats.rollup` were in `2d699d6`: the outer tenant walk is paged at `TenantFanOutLimit`, a run that fills a page enqueues a continuation carrying the keyset cursor rather than dropping the remaining tenants, and the per-tenant work (`fanOutTenant`'s bounded `ListDue` plus the two enqueues per due source) is unchanged. A test at the scale of the existing `internal/app/tenant_fanout_db_test.go` (or a sources-package equivalent) asserts that a tenant count above `TenantFanOutLimit` still reaches every tenant across successive ticks rather than being silently truncated at the first page.","files":null}]}